\section{What author inversion cannot do}{

Turning \code{"Massimo Aria"} into \code{"Aria, Massimo"} is a guess about which
tokens are the family name, and some of those guesses are wrong. The rules,
in order:
\itemize{
\item a name containing a comma is left alone - it is already inverted
\item a single token is left alone - a mononym or an organisation
\item a trailing \code{Jr}, \code{Sr}, \code{II}, \code{III} or \code{IV} stays with the family name
\item a \strong{lower-case} particle (\code{van}, \code{de}, \code{della}, \code{bin}, ...) before the
last token joins the family name, so \code{"Ludo van der Berg"} becomes
\code{"van der Berg, Ludo"}. The lower-case requirement is the rule BibTeX uses
to find the "von" part of a name; it means \code{"Della"} stays a given name.
}

The case it cannot detect is a name OpenAlex already stores family-name
first, which is common for Chinese and Korean authors: \code{"Wang Xiaoming"} is
indistinguishable from a given-name-first name and is inverted wrongly.
There is no signal in the data to separate the two, which is why
\code{invert_authors = FALSE} exists - it leaves every name exactly as OpenAlex
supplied it.
}

